+# AI adjudication boundary
+
+## Decision
+
+**BinaryRepair does not let a model's own words become the final success
+verdict.** The final authority is `app.domain.acceptance.decide_attempt_outcome`
+over V0–V5 evidence. AI may plan, propose, iterate and veto; it may not certify
+an unverified patch.
+
+This is not a temporary limitation to be removed by a stronger prompt. It is the
+only way the word `success_within_scope` remains meaningful.
+
+## What "AI final" can safely mean
+
+A fully autonomous loop is possible, but its final step must be one of:
+
+```text
+AI selects candidate
+  -> tool/guest applies it
+  -> deterministic obligations run
+  -> acceptance engine decides
+  -> AI writes a signed summary of that decision
+```
+
+The model can be the **final operator**:
+
+- choose what to inspect;
+- choose a candidate;
+- apply and re-run in the isolated guest;
+- continue repairing after failures;
+- stop and reject on its own;
+- write the final report.
+
+It cannot be the **final epistemic authority**:
+
+- no model-generated `success_within_scope`;
+- no model override of a failed/inconclusive obligation;
+- no model-supplied hash, evidence or token count;
+- no success when a required check is `not_run`.
+
+## One-way authority
+
+If product policy requires "AI has the final say", implement it as a **veto-only**
+authority:
+
+```text
+deterministic_verdict = accept(...)
+ai_decision = policy_model.assess(candidate, evidence)
+
+if ai_decision == "reject":
+    final = rejected_by_ai
+elif deterministic_verdict == success:
+    final = success_within_scope
+else:
+    final = deterministic_verdict
+```
+
+The AI can make the outcome stricter, never more permissive. This is safe,
+auditable and still allows fully autonomous repair loops.
+
+## Why raw model authority is rejected
+
+- A model can be prompt-injected by the binary, source, comments or tool output.
+- A model cannot prove that a required check actually ran.
+- A model can hallucinate hashes, test results or a `passed` obligation.
+- A model's confidence is not independent evidence.
+- A model failure must become `fallback`, `rejected` or `unsupported`, never
+  `success`.
+
+If a deployment truly wants raw model authority, it is a different product with
+different claims; it must not reuse BinaryRepair's V0–V5 acceptance label.
+
+## Required runtime guard for N-11
+
+N-11 is docs-only today. A real guard should, at the adjudication boundary:
+
+1. reject any AI payload containing a verdict/outcome/evidence field;
+2. require the verdict to be produced by the acceptance function, not parsed
+   from model text;
+3. verify the candidate hash and scope hash independently;
+4. record `model_calls` separately from `verdict`;
+5. fail closed if the evidence provider or guest is unavailable.
+
+Until that guard exists, do not describe the boundary document as enforced.
